add(id='rus-formation-outcrop', n='Rus Formation Outcrop (Sakhir Racecourse)', na='مكشف تكوين الرس (سباق الخيل)', c='geological', s='Early Eocene dolostone · studied 2023', lat=26.0725, lng=50.5220,
    p='Rus Formation, Early Eocene', dr='c. 56 – 48 million years ago', el='26.5 m section over 1.6 km²', ic='fossil', era='D', t0=56000000, t1=48000000,
    te='A lagoon turned to stone', ta='بحيرة ساحلية تحجّرت', se='Chalky dolostone with chert nodules and geodes, laid down in lagoons and tidal channels of a narrow Eocene strait', sa='دولوستون طباشيري بعقد الصوان والجيودات، ترسب في بحيرات ساحلية وقنوات مدّية لمضيق إيوسيني ضيق',
    desc="An outcrop of about 1.6 km² at the Horse Racing Club in central Bahrain, on the northern edge of the doubly plunging anticline, where a 26.5 m section of the Rus Formation is exposed; boreholes show the formation thickens to about 125 m in the west and 73 m in the east of the island. A 2023 study by Aimen Amer and colleagues logged 13 facies of dolostone, dolomitic marl and claystone with chert nodules near the top and calcite geodes in several beds; XRD showed 88% of 58 samples to be mainly dolomite, and only quartz pseudomorphs after gypsum and thin halite laminae remain of the evaporites. The beds record restricted lagoons, sabkha pools, tidal flats and channels and storm washovers along a shoreline trending NNW–SSE, with wind-blown quartz sand probably from the Arabian Shield. The authors argue that the Rus here is not mainly evaporitic and question its role as a regional hydrocarbon seal.",
    sig="The most detailed published section of the Rus Formation in Bahrain, the unit that holds the island's lowest aquifer and the geodes found across the interior.",
    ex='University of Turku (Amer, Skyttä, Ojala, Heilimo), published 2023',
    disc=['13 facies in a 26.5 m section', '88% of samples predominantly dolomite', 'Evidence of a narrow storm-swept Eocene strait'],
    refs=['Amer, A., Skyttä, P., Ojala, A. & Heilimo, E. (2023). New insights into the depositional facies of the Early Eocene Rus Formation, Bahrain. Marine and Petroleum Geology 154, 106338.', 'Willis, R. P. (1963). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. USGS Professional Paper 560-E.'])

add(id='jidda-island', n='Jidda Island', na='جزيرة جدة', c='geological', s='Limestone islet · quarry of the Barbar temple', lat=26.19, lng=50.41,
    p='Limestone islet of the western shelf', dr='Quarried c. 2500 BC', el='0.5 km² · cliffs to 10 m', ic='island', era='P', t0=4525, t1=0,
    te='The quarry across the water', ta='المحجر عبر الماء', se='A cliff-edged islet west of Bahrain whose stone is believed to have been shipped to build the Barbar temple', sa='جزيرة محاطة بجروف غرب البحرين يُعتقد أن حجرها نُقل بالقوارب لبناء معبد باربار',
    desc="Three small islets about 17.5 km west of Manama, just north of Umm an Nasan, with a total area of 0.5 km² and a highest point of 10 m; the main island consists of limestone cliffs. Rock blocks are believed to have been cut here and carried across the water to build the Barbar temple on the north coast of Bahrain Island, whose second temple was walled with carved quarried stone. The island is joined to Umm an Nasan by a 1.75 km causeway, is uninhabited and is closed to the public.",
    sig="The likely source of the dressed limestone of Bahrain's most important Dilmun temple, and a sample of the low limestone islets of the western shelf.",
    ex='No published geological survey; quarry link proposed by the Danish Barbar excavators',
    disc=['Believed source of the Barbar temple stone', 'Limestone cliffs rising to 10 m'],
    refs=['Wikipedia contributors (2026). Jidda Island.', 'Madain Project. Barbar Temple, citing Bibby (1969), Andersen & Højlund (2003) and Rice (1990).'])

add(id='causeway-pleistocene', n='Pleistocene Shelf of the King Fahd Causeway', na='رصيف البليستوسين عند جسر الملك فهد', c='geological', s='Ice-age sea-level record under the causeway', lat=26.17, lng=50.44,
    p='Pleistocene to Holocene', dr='c. 110,000 – 6,000 years ago', el='Dolomite up to 40 m · cover 3–9 m', ic='wave', era='D', t0=110000, t1=6000,
    te='When the Gulf ran dry', ta='حين جفّ الخليج', se='Boreholes for the causeway cut through dolomite and sands recording the emptying and refilling of the Arabian Gulf', sa='اخترقت حفريات الجسر دولوميت ورمالًا تسجل جفاف الخليج العربي وامتلاءه من جديد',
    desc="Sediments studied along the King Fahd Causeway between Bahrain and Saudi Arabia by Ata Darwish and Curtis Conley (1990). A lower dolomite interval up to 40 m thick is built of repeated cycles of skeletal packstone, mudstone, pelletal grainstone and green mudstone, dated to the mid-Pleistocene before the first great sea-level fall of 110,000–90,000 years ago. Above it a 3–9 m interval of quartz sandstone, skeletal grainstone, quartz sand and aragonitic oolitic sand records a transgression of 90,000–75,000 years ago, a late Pleistocene blanket of dune sand as aridity grew, and the Flandrian flooding of the Gulf about 6,000 years ago. During the lowstand the whole Gulf was emptied and the sediments were leached, cemented and dolomitised; in wetter periods fresh water dissolved aragonite and precipitated calcite.",
    sig="A measured record of how the shallow Gulf floor between Bahrain and Arabia dried out and flooded with the ice ages.",
    ex='Darwish & Conley (King Abdulaziz University), 1990; recent sands noted by the USGS survey of 1963',
    disc=['Gulf emptied entirely in the late Pleistocene regression', 'Reflooded about 6,000 years ago'],
    refs=['Darwish, A. & Conley, C. D. (1990). Pleistocene-Holocene sedimentation and diagenesis along the King Fahd causeway between Saudi Arabia and Bahrain. Journal of King Abdulaziz University: Earth Sciences 3(1), 63–79.', 'Willis, R. P. (1963). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. USGS Professional Paper 560-E.'])

