    desc="A patch of 0.157 km² of abandoned, privately owned date-palm scrub and open ground at 2–5 m elevation in the village of Maqabah, about 10 km from Manama, bounded by roads, farms and housing. Scattered date palms cover about 60% of it. BirdLife International identified it as an Important Bird Area, last assessed in 1994, as the main roost in Bahrain of the grey hypocolius (Hypocolius ampelinus), a passage and wintering bird that feeds by day in palm gardens elsewhere on the island. It has no legal protection, though it has been proposed as a wildlife reserve, and faces cutting of palm scrub, disturbance of the roosts, rubbish and encroaching housing.",
